;; talk/private--canon.scm — objections to surfaces/private.scm from the lens of canon.

(objection 'o-private-canon-1 :by "sonnet-canon" :to 'private-default-pay-kind :lens "canon"
  :claim (canon 'htlc-constants-are-simnet "The default way a private person pays anyone is the held (hash-locked) payment, and the pay flow signs it. The queue says no real-funds hash-locked payment on an EVM mainnet until per-jurisdiction expiry values are decided, so on that chain the default payment cannot carry real money. The person's first and most frequent job is then either blocked or quietly served by the direct kind, which the default limits to a company the person already has an account with. d-private-refuses mentions refusing real money only where real funds are not allowed; it does not say that the default itself is what is refused. The design assumes the queue item closes. A maintainer decision on what Pay is on an EVM mainnet before that is needed, and none exists in this file."))

(objection 'o-private-canon-2 :by "sonnet-canon" :to 'd-private-what-is-certain :lens "canon"
  :claim (canon 'credit-secured-unsecured "Only collateral-backed entitlement is secured. The leaning certain-above-cost counts a locked share as resting on the company's word when it is smaller than the cost of leaving, which makes the person's first-screen 'mine for sure' number differ from the protocol's secured figure and differ from what the other surfaces call secured. d-kernel-secured already asks the maintainer this same question and leans the opposite way (locked-share, with the cost of leaving as its own number), so this decision is a second, conflicting decision on one word, and it is duplicated across surfaces. Its stated basis is a guess about docs/rejected-ideas.md, not a quote, and rejected-ideas-need-new-evidence requires evidence. The cost would be a private person shown a smaller 'for sure' figure than the runtime would state, and a runtime that must keep a second definition of secured."))

(objection 'o-private-canon-3 :by "sonnet-canon" :to 'private-savings :lens "canon"
  :claim (canon 'lending-reopened "Lending must appear with exact money, who holds authority, deadlines and recovery. The savings screen shows principal, rate, interest so far and term only. The late window is a figure that exists and is not shown; nothing on the screen says who can end the deal or what the person does if the company does not repay on time, and the screen offers no leaving or recovery path from it. The person is told what they earn and not what protects them when the term passes. The screen answers when they can have the money back, but as written cannot say what happens if they cannot."))

(objection 'o-private-canon-4 :by "sonnet-canon" :to 'private-protection :lens "canon"
  :claim (canon 'recovery-coverage-visible "The release queue lists, as an item gating public launch, an account-level recovery coverage view with tower receipt and failure visibility. The protection screen answers whether the person could get their money back after losing the phone and shows nothing (:shows is empty). private-needs-copy-kept asks the base to say whether the last copy was accepted or failed, but no screen shows the answer, so the person sees a promise and not the receipt or the failure. Per errors-are-shown, a failed copy that does not reach the person is a swallowed failure. The cost is a person who believes they are covered when the last copy never arrived."))

(objection 'o-private-canon-5 :by "sonnet-canon" :to 'd-private-refuses :lens "canon"
  :claim (canon 'capacity-bar-and-pro-toggle "The queue asks for one capacity bar and a Pro toggle on by default in both wallets, and the constraint says a design that hides detail should use that toggle instead of inventing its own. The leaning absent-here deletes the detail for this person and gives them no way to see it. The decision's own text says this conflicts with the toggle and needs the user's word, but the decider is the user. A queue item and repository canon are the maintainer's to bend, and d-kernel-how-many already lists a one-surface-with-Pro-switch option, so the question is shared with the kernel and is open there. Nothing in the file says the maintainer waived the toggle for the private face. Until then the leaning assumes it will. A person who needs one refused action has to find a second wallet."))

(objection 'o-private-canon-6 :by "sonnet-canon" :to 'private-no-borrowing :lens "canon"
  :claim (canon 'launch-flows "The wallets together must offer Lending on EVM and native TRON, and a spec with several surfaces must say which surface carries each flow on each chain. The gap says another surface must carry borrowing but does not name it. This file never names TRON except in one :changes-if line, so private never says which chains savings is carried on. Whether borrowing is carried anywhere depends on other files, and nothing here states what happens to the lending launch flow if none takes it. The cost is that the one lending direction this surface drops could be silently left out of launch."))
